  "editorial_note": "GitHub listed fourteen projects this Sunday morning. Ten had been featured before, and none showed a change big enough to bring it back, so all four picks are first-time entries. Orkas leads as ==a desktop app that runs a team of AI agents==, including coding agents you already use. The other three widen the mix: Vorssaint, a free macOS menu bar toolkit; OpenMAIC, a self-hosted AI classroom from a university group; and a long-running DevOps question bank. ==Two of the four picks are not AI tools==. Curation order reflects practical interest, not GitHub rank. Trending is not a release date or an endorsement.",
  "coverage_notes": [
    "All 14 listing entries were inspected. Exact primary public GitHub repository metadata and recent releases were checked for all 14 at about 07:01 UTC, and README files for the four picks; all were active and unarchived. Thirteen had an API-detected license; for bregman-arie/devops-exercises the API reports NOASSERTION, so we read its LICENSE file, which is Creative Commons legal text granting non-commercial sharing without adaptations.",
    "Ten entries featured before were left out without a material change: mattpocock/skills, pingdotgg/t3code and lightpanda-io/browser (31 October), thedotmack/claude-mem, Donchitos/Claude-Code-Game-Studios and milind-soni/OpenMausBot (30 October), HKUDS/DeepTutor and obra/superpowers (28 October), pbakaus/impeccable (25 October) and Renhuai123/ziwei-doushu (24 October). Ziwei-doushu falls just outside the seven-day window, but its latest release (15 October) predates its card.",
    "Releases since earlier cards, noted here rather than repeated: thedotmack/claude-mem v15.8.0 (1 November) adds a cloud-hosted version of its My Work page behind sign-in, and makes work-state data (to-do lists, focus areas, rules) sync to its cloud hub for every cloud-sync account whose devices are new enough (pull request 5193); this matters only to users of its cloud sync. pingdotgg/t3code v0.1.6 (31 October, after yesterday's card) adds side chats and 1Password credentials and keeps connection tickets and OAuth credentials out of traces. HKUDS/DeepTutor v1.7.1 (31 October) adds desktop preview builds for Mac and Windows.",
    "Today's listing again had no security projects. We did not reach outside the daily listing to add one.",
    "The preceding seven calendar dates, 25-31 October, were checked for local history; all seven editions exist.",
    "The official listing was captured at 08:00:34 Europe/Paris. Literal stars-today labels are GitHub's displayed period figures; API total stars and README evidence were checked separately at the times shown per card. They are not an independently audited rolling 24-hour growth measurement.",
    "Feature, privacy and cost descriptions are the maintainers' own, including Orkas's offline and data-handling statements, Vorssaint's privacy notes and the devops-exercises question count. No candidate code, installer, plugin, skill or model was installed, downloaded or run, and none of this is a security audit or endorsement."
  ],
  "projects": [
    {
      "repository": "Orkas-AI/Orkas",
      "url": "https://github.com/Orkas-AI/Orkas",
      "trending_rank": 11,
      "description": "A ==local-first desktop app that runs a team of AI agents==. You give a Commander agent a goal; it handles simple work itself and, for bigger jobs, splits it into to-dos for 16 built-in specialists (research, writing, slide decks, video, office files, UI and code, e-commerce) and for coding or personal agents you already have installed, such as Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, OpenClaw and Hermes-Agent. Projects keep goals, rules, memory and files between chats, and automations can repeat tasks on a schedule.",
      "why_interesting": "First time on our list. Most agent tools focus on one assistant or on code; Orkas ==coordinates several existing CLI agents under one plan== and adds a project to-do list that people and agents share, with an Auto-advance mode that picks up the next item and returns finished work for review. The source build has no account layer and, by the README's account, can run fully offline against a local model endpoint.",
      "caveat": "The free source build lacks shared projects, cloud sync and web-app publishing, which are ==only in the packaged app, which requires sign-in==; its built-in model runs on Orkas's managed service with 500 free credits, then paid plans from $11.99 a month or your own provider keys. Voice input sends audio to Orkas. The first source launch downloads Python, Node, FFmpeg, whisper.cpp and an embedding model, and ==the agents it drives can act on your files and accounts==. We did not install or run it.",
      "category": "AI agents / desktop orchestration",
      "language": "JavaScript",
      "license": "MIT",
      "stars_total": 6880,
      "stars_period": 1044,
      "stars_period_label": "1,044 stars today",
