    {
      "repository": "THU-MAIC/OpenMAIC",
      "url": "https://github.com/THU-MAIC/OpenMAIC",
      "trending_rank": 12,
      "description": "OpenMAIC (Open Multi-Agent Interactive Classroom) ==turns a topic or a document into an interactive lesson== led by AI teacher and classmate agents. It generates slides, quizzes, HTML simulations and project-based activities, draws on a whiteboard and speaks with text-to-speech. Lessons export as a PowerPoint file, a single offline HTML file or a ZIP for another server. It is self-hosted and works with many model providers, including local ones through Ollama.",
      "why_interesting": "First time on our list. It is an academic project from the THU-MAIC group, which gives a Tsinghua University contact and links a 2026 journal paper, yet it ships like a product: v1.3.0 on 29 October added a per-user knowledge base for uploaded materials. Its changelog also shows ==published security advisories and fixes for SSRF and DNS rebinding==, a good sign for a server that fetches documents and calls many APIs.",
      "caveat": "Self-hosting needs Node 22.19+, pnpm and PostgreSQL 16, plus model API keys; the README recommends turning on images, narration, video and web search, which ==multiplies paid API calls per lesson==. Generated lessons can contain model errors, so a teacher still needs to check them. The root licence is MIT, but the bundled mathml2omml package is LGPL-3.0-or-later. We did not run it.",
      "category": "AI / education",
      "language": "TypeScript",
      "license": "MIT",
